  "heading": null,
  "heading_latex": "\\textrm{Transitivity of Subset Relation}",
  "body_text": null,
  "proof_text": "Assume $A \\subseteq B$ and $B \\subseteq C$. Let $x \\in A$ be an\narbitrary element. By definition of $A \\subseteq B$, the condition\n$x \\in A$ implies $x \\in B$. Since $x \\in B$, the condition\n$B \\subseteq C$ similarly implies $x \\in C$. Because $x \\in A$ was\narbitrary, every element of $A$ belongs to $C$, which establishes\n$A \\subseteq C$.",
  "proof_source": "Proof: \"\"\"Assume $A \\subseteq B$ and $B \\subseteq C$. Let $x \\in A$ be an\n          arbitrary element. By definition of $A \\subseteq B$, the condition\n          $x \\in A$ implies $x \\in B$. Since $x \\in B$, the condition\n          $B \\subseteq C$ similarly implies $x \\in C$. Because $x \\in A$ was\n          arbitrary, every element of $A$ belongs to $C$, which establishes\n          $A \\subseteq C$.\"\"\"",
  "page": null,
  "source": "Theorem:\ngiven:\n. C is \\set\n. B \\:subset:/ C\n. A \\:subset:/ B\nthen:\n. A \\:subset?:/ C\nDocumented:\n. called: \"Transitivity of Subset Relation\"\n. description: \"If $A \\subseteq B$ and $B \\subseteq C$, then $A \\subseteq C$.\"\nProof: \"\"\"Assume $A \\subseteq B$ and $B \\subseteq C$. Let $x \\in A$ be an\n          arbitrary element. By definition of $A \\subseteq B$, the condition\n          $x \\in A$ implies $x \\in B$. Since $x \\in B$, the condition\n          $B \\subseteq C$ similarly implies $x \\in C$. Because $x \\in A$ was\n          arbitrary, every element of $A$ belongs to $C$, which establishes\n          $A \\subseteq C$.\"\"\"\nId: \"6a4bcb50-97bc-4d1b-a9b2-750c963bb720\"",
